---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'; import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro'; import FeatureCard from '../components/FeatureCard.astro'; import ScreenshotGallery from '../components/ScreenshotGallery.astro'; import CallToAction from '../components/CallToAction.astro';
const structuredData={ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'SoftwareApplication',name:'FlowCast',description:'Self-hosted radio automation, scheduling and playout platform.',url:'https://flowcast.spotycast.ovh',applicationCategory:'MultimediaApplication',operatingSystem:'Linux amd64',softwareRequirements:'Docker Engine and Docker Compose v2',codeRepository:'https://github.com/chourmovs/FlowCast-Community',downloadUrl:'https://github.com/chourmovs/FlowCast-Community/releases',license:'https://github.com/chourmovs/FlowCast-Community/blob/main/LICENSE' };
---
<BaseLayout title="FlowCast — Self-hosted radio automation and playout" description="Run radio scheduling, audio analysis and automated Icecast playout on an operator-managed Linux host with FlowCast." structuredData={structuredData}><Hero/><section class="page container"><p class="eyebrow">ONE EXPLICIT BROADCAST PATH</p><h2>Built around the work of running a station</h2><div class="grid"><FeatureCard title="Dedicated Rust engine">Playout receives explicit station configuration and executes the broadcast without a separate Liquidsoap scripting layer.</FeatureCard><FeatureCard title="Configurable transitions">Tune fades, overlap, bridge duration, track exits, queues and prefetch behaviour.</FeatureCard><FeatureCard title="BPM-aware scheduling">Tempo can inform selection alongside editorial playlist rules—it does not replace them.</FeatureCard></div></section><section class="page container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">PURPOSE-BUILT PLAYOUT</p><h2>Configuration, not generated scripts</h2><p class="intro">FlowCast’s scheduler, analysis and Rust playout engine are designed as one system. Operators express station behaviour through explicit controls rather than maintaining a domain-specific Liquidsoap script.</p><p>This reduces layers, but it is a deliberate trade-off: FlowCast does not offer Liquidsoap’s general-purpose scripting flexibility. <a href="/liquidsoap-alternative">Understand the architecture and limits</a>.</p></div><div class="panel"><h2>Community stays autonomous</h2><p>Community provides the authenticated control, scheduling, analysis, playout and Icecast path. It starts and broadcasts without a paid licence or mandatory remote licence-service request.</p><p>Optional Pro capabilities are separately licensed. <a href="/community-vs-pro">Compare documented boundaries</a>.</p></div></section><section class="page container"><p class="eyebrow">REAL INTERFACE</p><h2>See the control plane</h2><ScreenshotGallery limit={2}/><p><a href="/screenshots">Explore every screenshot and caption →</a></p></section><section class="page container split"><div><h2>Security and operations</h2><p>FlowCast supplies diagnostics, backup, restore, controlled update and rollback tooling. Credentials remain local. Operators remain responsible for host hardening, TLS and backups.</p></div><div class="notice"><strong>Docker socket warning</strong><p>Optional Docker Control mounts the Docker socket, which is equivalent to root-level host control. Restrict access and use <code>--no-docker-control</code> to opt out.</p><a href="/installation">Read the installation safety notes</a></div></section><CallToAction/></BaseLayout>
